# (paragraph prefix in v10, new text). {n;m} = one citation control with the numbers of v10.
PARS = [
("ILDFIRE response", """ILDFIRE response is carried out by a system of systems {1}. Satellites and weather services deliver observations. A hazard model turns them into an expected fire. Regional teams decide where to commit crews, aircraft, and evacuation orders. An operator supervises the whole under a clock set by the fire {2;3}. Fire seasons are becoming longer and more severe {2}. Moreover, the decisions that matter most are taken in the first hours. In these hours observations are sparse, the forecast is uncertain, and a wrong commitment is most costly {3;4}. A recommendation that arrives after the fire has moved is useless. Hence, the support has to adapt to the incident as it moves."""),

("DSSs exist for parts", """DSSs exist for parts of this task {5;6}. Spread simulators forecast fire behaviour from fuel, terrain, and weather {7;8}. Allocation tools assign crews and aircraft against a forecast {9;10}. Integrated platforms bring these parts together for use in the field {4;11;12}. However, field studies of these tools measure how widely they are used, not whether the decisions taken with them are better {13;14}. A critical review of DSS research reports the same pattern across the field. Many systems are built, and few are shown to help {15}."""),

("Three structural limits", """Three structural limits repeat across these systems. First, the alternatives are predefined. A rule base, an optimisation model, or a menu of tactics fixes the set of orders before the incident. Hence, the system can rank the alternatives it is given, but it cannot propose what the incident needs {11;16;17}. Decision analysis has long identified the generation of alternatives as the weakest step of the process {18;19;20}. The methods proposed for it either enumerate variations of a fixed model {21;22} or depend on the recall of the operator under time pressure {23}. Second, uncertainty is representable only through prior distributions or belief intervals. Real-time sensing cannot supply them {24;25}. Furthermore, the reasoning behind a recommendation is exposed, if at all, through post-hoc attribution {26}. Third, integrated platforms embed the forecast and the decision logic in one tool {4;11}. Therefore, neither is validated on its own, and the benefit of a decision mechanism cannot be separated from the quality of the forecast behind it. In other words, the decision space of current systems is closed. It is fixed before the incident, and it cannot be verified or extended during it."""),

("DisasterAware answers these limits", """DisasterAware answers these limits with an architecture whose central contribution is an open decision space. The set of orders the system may issue is not fixed in advance. It grows during the incident, under verification by the hazard model. The architecture separates two cores. A simulation core owns the hazard state and is validated against satellite records. A decision core reads the state through confidence-rated observations only. The decision core has two layers. The closed layer is a self-tuning fuzzy doctrine. Bounded observation features aggregate into decision concepts, a sparse rule base maps the concepts to intervention families, and an evolving mechanism tunes consequents and term boundaries while the vocabulary stays fixed. The open layer sits on top of it. When the standing decision falls short, a pretrained large language model (LLM) proposes a new object. The object is a new rule, an intermediate concept, a blend of existing interventions, or an intervention composed from physical effects the simulator can execute. Nothing enters the doctrine unless it passes a chain of algebraic gates and a paired forecast test on independently seeded futures."""),

("This article asks what the open layer", """This article asks what the open layer is worth to a commander. It answers with paired measurement. The same fires are replayed with no decision support, with the closed layer alone, and with the two layers together. Hence, the benefit of opening the decision space is read from the difference it makes on identical worlds. Three contributions follow. First, the open decision space is defined as an architectural object. It is the set of orders a system may issue, together with the rules under which that set may be extended. The invariants this object preserves are stated and proved for the gate chain of Section IV. Second, the extension mechanism is specified to the level of its grammar and geometry. A proposal is a tuple over a closed basis of verified effects, sectors, and distance bands, and the simulator interprets it through a fixed operator algebra. Thus, the space is closed at effects and open at interventions. Third, the architecture is measured end to end on an independently validated hazard model, over paired worlds drawn from several scenario families. Every proposal, gate verdict, and admitted object is logged. Therefore, the question of what was learned can be answered object by object rather than only in aggregate. The contribution is architectural rather than algorithmic. The concept-driven inference and the generative proposer are replaceable components, and the invariants are properties of the gates. Section II reviews related work. Section III presents the architecture and Section IV the decision core. Section V reports the results, Section VI discusses them, and Section VII concludes."""),

("Surface fire spread is described", """Surface fire spread is described by Rothermel-type rate-of-spread models driven by fuel, moisture, wind, and slope {7;27;28}. These models are implemented in operational tools such as FlamMap {8} and in cellular simulators such as Cell2Fire {29}. Decision support built on these models ranges from strategic risk assessment {4;9} and multi-criteria allocation {10;16;30} to the integrated WFDSS platform {11}. The field use of WFDSS has been studied extensively {12;13;14}. Across these systems the decision logic is authored offline and the intervention menu is fixed. Moreover, the evaluation reports the fidelity of the hazard model or the usability of the tool, not the change in the decision itself {14;15}. Fire management economics frames the decision as cost plus loss {3;31}. This framing is the basis of the cost used here."""),

("Decision analysis has long assumed", """Decision analysis has long assumed that the set of alternatives is given before evaluation begins {32;33}. Arbel and Tong {18} identified option generation as a neglected phase that precedes choice. Colorni and Tsoukias {19;34} formalized the construction of the alternative set as a recursion of decision problems. Hofstede {20} observed that planning systems which hard-wire the open aspects of a problem are not adopted. Cognitive evidence points the same way. Option generation in ill-structured problems depends on ideation beyond memory retrieval. Furthermore, generating more options raises the quality of the best option while it lowers the mean quality of the set {35}. Commanders build courses of action from recognized patterns rather than from enumerated menus {23}. These works motivate a DSS that generates alternatives during operation. They also set the condition under which generation is safe. In other words, broad generation must be paired with strict, external evaluation."""),

("Three families of mechanisms", """Three families of mechanisms open a decision space. The first searches a fixed parameter space for structurally different solutions, as in the generator-evaluator separation of active decision support {22} and modeling to generate alternatives {21}. The second composes alternatives from modules under compatibility constraints {17;36}. The third extends the action model at run time, as in open-world planning and action discovery {37;38}. In all three the opened space stays bounded by a basis the system already understands. Hence, the open space is an open composition over a closed and verified basis. This is the sense used here. Two design consequences follow. Generation must be cheap to reject, because most of the composition space leaves the outcome unchanged. Moreover, the evaluator must be independent of the generator. Evolving Takagi-Sugeno (TS) models create and tune rules online within the existing vocabulary {39;40}. Value-based selectors learn when to change structure {41}. Generative models draft rules outside the current structure {42}. DisasterAware places the first inside the closed layer and the last inside the open layer, behind one satisficing acceptance rule {43}."""),

("Recent wildfire and disaster systems", """Recent wildfire and disaster systems have added learned and generative components. However, the intervention space has stayed closed or unchecked. Dubey and Dubey {44} couple a Transformer forecaster with fifteen expert rules that map predicted risk to four fixed response levels. Chandra et al. {45} draw suggested actions from a predefined ontology and check them for consistency only. Cheng et al. {46} let LLMs weight a fixed hierarchy of indicators. Gerolimos et al. {47} call for open-world learning in disaster management. In their case the openness concerns the recognition of unseen event classes, while the response stays a mapping to familiar actions. At the other end, multi-agent LLMs produce free-text response plans. Their hallucinations are contained by retrieval grounding, output schemas, or post-hoc grading {48;49}. Cui et al. {50} conclude that external verification through physics-based simulation is required. Nevertheless, they confine their own agent to a fixed tool library. WildfireKR {51} is the closest neighbour. It is an LLM agent that automates calibrated spread simulation. However, it produces suppression-free envelopes and leaves intervention design to the analyst. The gap that remains is a system in which the intervention space is open at run time. In such a system every new intervention is built from effects a physical model can execute. Moreover, admission is decided by the physical model rather than by the proposer, by a schema, or by the decision maker alone."""),

("Operational assessments arrive", """Operational assessments arrive as graded, linguistic judgments. Their formal counterpart is partial membership {52}. Domain knowledge enters as rules of the canonical conjunctive form. Zero-order TS inference turns rule activations into a crisp intensity as a firing-strength weighted average of constant consequents {39}. The choice is architectural. Candidates are judged inside shadow forecasts that run many times per incident, so centroid integration would be too costly. Moreover, the outputs are intensity commands, and adaptation needs numeric consequents with predictable effect while antecedents stay linguistic. Applying inference directly over a rich observation vector is combinatorially infeasible. In fact, ten features with five terms each span nearly ten million antecedent combinations. A conceptual middle layer reduces this to five decision concepts. Each concept answers exactly once one of the questions commanders ask {3;23}. Thus, the antecedent space shrinks to 3125 combinations, and only the active subset is instantiated. Furthermore, the concepts double as the vocabulary in which every recommendation is explained."""),

("Fig. 1 presents DisasterAware", """Fig. 1 presents DisasterAware as an SoS with five kinds of participant. External providers supply weather, imagery, terrain, and resource status. A simulation core owns the hazard state. N local decision agents are each responsible for one region. A coordination layer allocates capacity without performing inference. A commander supervises through a dashboard. Participants meet only at declared interfaces. The simulation core is authoritative. Cells carry burning status, fuel mass, an intensity proxy, and time since ignition, and no other participant may write this state. The decision agents read derived observations. The dashboard admits only reversible, input-level actions such as pause, rollback, ignition injection, and resource changes."""),

("The landscape is a rectangular grid", """The landscape is a rectangular grid. The state at step k is the collection of cell states, each with burning status, fuel mass, intensity proxy, and time since ignition. The transition operator Φ advances the state from the external input set. This set collects meteorological forcing, topography, fuel conditions, ignition injection, and the intervention parameters (1). A separate contextual set guides decision generation only and never touches the fire dynamics. It consists of a value layer, which aggregates buildings, critical facilities, population, and evacuation access, and a resource layer of available capabilities."""),

("A cell burns through persistence", """A cell burns through persistence while fuel remains, through propagation from burning neighbours, or through external ignition. Propagation fires when the influence accumulated over the eight-connected neighbourhood exceeds a calibrated threshold. Each neighbour contributes a Rothermel-type rate of spread from wind, slope, aspect, moisture, and fuel type {7}, weighted toward the wind. Ember spotting is the only stochastic element. It carries fire several cells downwind and across narrow barriers. Precipitation raises dead fuel moisture above the extinction level, so a front dies out gradually. Fuel mass decreases through combustion and suppression, never below zero. Suppression converts capability into a normalized fuel reduction. The reduction is gated by capacity and availability, decays with travel time and poor access, and is attenuated by local fire intensity. Hence, suppression never overwrites the burning state. It acts on the fuel that shapes persistence and propagation."""),

("The decision layer reads the state", """The decision layer reads the state through the observation interface only, as in (2). The observation function h projects the state onto the cells of a region. It injects epistemic uncertainty at this point and nowhere else. The aggregated intervention field re-enters the simulation as one component of the external input set. Per-step snapshots that include the random number generator enable exact counterfactual replay. Runs from the same snapshot differ only in the orders under test. This is the basis of every paired comparison in Section V."""),

("Fig. 2 presents one local agent", """Fig. 2 presents one local agent. Perception reduces the heterogeneous inputs to ten bounded features with explicit confidence. The concept space abstracts them. Two decision layers generate, adapt, and evaluate interventions. N agents run this loop over their own regions. A coordination layer ranks priorities and shares capacity among them."""),

("Each agent receives the region-restricted", """Each agent receives the region-restricted observation and computes ten features in the unit interval, namely fire intensity, spread potential, weather severity, ignition proximity, fuel load, asset exposure, resource accessibility, access and road status, suppression availability, and temporal urgency. Every observed cell carries a confidence composed from observability, coverage, freshness, and reliability. The composition takes their minimum rather than their average. The magnitude of the observation error is bounded by the complement of the confidence (3). Hence, full confidence is exact and vanishing confidence admits the worst case. No distribution over the error is assumed. The dominant uncertainty is epistemic. The state is under-observed rather than noisy {24}. Bayesian or Dempster-Shafer treatments presuppose distributional knowledge that real-time sensing lacks {25}."""),

("The concept space maps physically", """The concept space maps physically related features into six base concepts, the base concepts into five decision concepts, and finally into one coordination concept. The activation of a base concept is the weighted sum of the term memberships of its features. The activation of every higher concept is the weighted sum of the activations below it. All weights are nonnegative and normalized to one (4). Raw activations are never consumed. Every activation is gated by its confidence and blended with a persistence-carried prior, as in (5). A reliable observation passes unchanged. An uninformative one falls back to the decayed previous estimate. An unobserved concept fades geometrically. The transition is linear with no threshold to tune, and the activation snaps back as soon as confidence returns. The five decision concepts are fire threat level, suppression feasibility, asset exposure risk, intervention urgency, and evacuation pressure. They are the only variables over which rules are written. Moreover, they double as the traceability vocabulary."""),

("Decision rules are written over", """Decision rules are written over the five gated concepts in the canonical conjunctive form. Each antecedent clause tests one concept against one linguistic term of a five-term partition of unity. The consequent assigns a singleton intensity to each of six intervention families, namely suppression effort, resource deployment, containment line, asset protection, evacuation, and public warning. Inaction is the zero-intensity point of every family. Hence, the cost prices restraint as it prices action. A rule fires with the strength of its weakest satisfied clause. The candidate is assembled family by family from the rules that fire (6). The denominator is floored at one, so a weakly supported conclusion yields a weak order. The base is sparse. Of 3125 antecedent combinations it holds the seeded doctrine and what adaptation adds. A run starts from five seed rules, one per family, with suppression feasibility acting as gatekeeper. Below a coverage floor of 0.05 the layer falls back to a watchful posture and adaptation is triggered."""),

("Every candidate is judged on a shadow", """Every candidate is judged on a shadow copy of the simulation before anything reaches the field. The decision cost is the weighted five-term sum over burned area, asset loss, population exposure, response effort, and response delay, each normalized by a scenario reference, as in (7). Burned area enters through a rational form that keeps every saved cell worth saving. Population enters as person-steps inside burning cells. Response enters as the time integral of committed force. Delay is measured against a sixty-minute reference. The weights are 1, 1, 1, 0.2, 0.2. The first three terms form the physical cost J_phys. This is the quantity every cross-configuration comparison reads, because a run that never intervenes carries no response cost. The candidate is held constant over a forecast horizon and compared with a no-action shadow run from the same state. Acceptance is satisficing, as in (8). The bound is the tighter of a fixed ceiling and a required relative gain over inaction. Hence, cheap inaction demands a stronger candidate. Failing the test leaves the standing orders in force while adaptation runs."""),

("A quality measure catches", """A quality measure catches what the cost cannot. For each scored concept the gated activation is compared with the strongest intensity the candidate assigns to the family that answers it. The quality is one minus the mean mismatch. Below the quality gate the graduated fail-safe attenuates the candidate toward a pre-authorized conservative posture, in proportion to the deficit. The offensive families are scaled down. However, evacuation and public warning are never reduced. If the adapted candidate is still forecast worse than inaction, a no-harm rule withholds the offensive allocation over a lookahead."""),

("The closed layer adapts itself", """The closed layer adapts itself without changing its vocabulary, in the evolving fuzzy tradition {40}. Two bounded moves act on rules already in the base. The consequent step nudges one intensity by 0.05 within the unit interval. The boundary shift moves the shared edge of two neighbouring terms while it preserves the partition of unity. A third move grows the base inside the vocabulary. On an uncovered combination of the two most active concepts it writes a rule. Where a covered combination is ambiguous, it inserts a narrow term at the crisp reading. Every trial acts on a working copy and is kept only when the forecast physical cost strictly falls (9). This layer is the state of the art in self-tuning fuzzy decision support. Therefore, it is the reference against which the open layer is measured."""),

("The open layer adds knowledge", """The open layer adds knowledge that neither tuning nor instantiation can produce. It is entered when the standing decision demonstrably falls short. In detail, the triggers are a forecast cost above the satisficing bound, a coverage gap while fire is alive, or renewed spread under applied orders. A value-based selector with a contextual-bandit update decides whether the closed layer tries again or the open layer is called. Its inputs are the deficit and the coverage verdict, and its reward is the forecast cost reduction obtained."""),

("The last form is what makes", """The last form is what makes the space open at interventions while closed at effects, as depicted in Fig. 3. A clause names a sector and a band. The engine resolves the band as the set of cells whose four-neighbour distance to the reference core of the sector lies in the band. Head, flank, and rear are split by the cosine of the angle between the direction from the fire centroid and the mean wind (10). Admissibility masks then remove cells. They remove cells a crew cannot reach for wet, clear, and ignite, built-up and water cells for clear and ignite, and unpopulated cells for evacuate and prime. The surviving cells receive an idempotent update of the engine field the effect writes, at the amount scaled by the firing intensity of the rule, as in (11). Coating raises the retardant field. Wetting and clearing raise committed capacity. Drafting multiplies capacity already committed in proportion to the distance to water. Evacuation and priming raise the evacuation and warning fields. Every update takes a maximum, so two orders on the same cell never add. Furthermore, the three library channels of the seed doctrine (retardant drop, water drafting, tactical burn) are themselves the operators coat, draft, and ignite with fixed masks. Therefore, a proposal can recompose a known tactic, re-site it, or build a new one, but it cannot invent physics. Whatever it builds is measured on the same engine as the doctrine it extends."""),

("Every proposal is untrusted", """Every proposal is untrusted by construction and passes the gate chain of Fig. 4. Five algebraic gates run without a simulation step. G1 checks canonical form. G2 checks vocabulary, ranges, and duplication. G2b checks that a new concept is not redundant. The cosine between its weight vector and every existing one must stay below 0.95. G2c checks relevance. The proposed rule must fire on the current reading. G2d checks pool economy. The requested capacity must not exceed the pool. Four simulation gates follow. G3 and G4 demand that the forecast physical cost with the proposal strictly fall below the forecast without it on two independently seeded rollouts (12). G5 requires a package, a rule carrying a new object, to clear an additional margin δ. G5b requires the package to beat its own rule stripped of the new object, as in (13). Hence, a package is rejected when its gain belongs to the ordinary orders written beside the new object. A rejection returns the gate name and both forecast values to the proposer. The proposer may revise once, and a second rejection ends the attempt. An admitted object enters the rule base, is tagged with its origin, and is logged with its gate values. Therefore, every later order traces to the deficit that triggered it and to the test that admitted it."""),

("The coordinator performs no inference", """The coordinator performs no inference. Each agent sends up its operational priority, its resource request, and a confidence summary. It receives back the allocation, the priority weights, and the acceptance thresholds in force. Regions are ranked by operational priority and receive an attention share. A region near the top keeps the full share. A monitored region receives a reduced share floored at 0.5 and a stricter quality threshold for offensive orders. Evacuation and warning are untouched. The merged plan is forecast by the no-harm guard against inaction. When the plan is worse, offensive orders are withheld while life-safety orders stand."""),

("The gates give the architecture", """The gates give the architecture four properties that hold whatever produced the candidate. The reason is that they are properties of the acceptance function and not of the proposer. Proposition 1 (no-harm). No order reaches the field whose forecast physical cost exceeds the no-action forecast over the guard horizon. Indeed, acceptance admits a candidate only below the satisficing bound, and the no-harm rule withholds the offensive allocation otherwise. Proposition 2 (life-safety invariance). Evacuation and warning intensities are never reduced by any gate. Neither the fail-safe nor the no-harm rule has the two families in its domain, and a proposal that would lower them fails G2. Proposition 3 (coverage). At every reachable reading either some rule fires above the coverage floor, or the layer falls back to the watchful posture and triggers adaptation. Proposition 4 (monotone descent and termination). Along any sequence of admissions the forecast physical cost on the first seed decreases by at least δ per admitted package. Hence, the number of admissions in one incident is bounded by Ĵ(R_0)/δ, and the extension halts once no proposal clears the gates. A fifth property is probabilistic rather than exact. A proposal whose true expected effect is zero or harmful passes both G3 and G4 with probability at most q². Here q is the probability that one shadow forecast reports a gain by chance. Thus, the two-seed test bounds false admission without ensemble forecasting. Section V-E reports how often admitted objects paid off in the realized outcome."""),

("Eight criteria fixed in advance", """Eight criteria fixed in advance are audited at the end of this section, namely predictive fidelity, decision quality, timeliness, robustness, uncertainty handling, adaptability, explainability, and scalability, listed in Table I. Predictive fidelity is tested by blind hindcast. The simulation core receives only the real inputs of a documented fire, with no tuning on that fire. Its output is compared with the satellite record. Terrain comes from Copernicus GLO-30, fuel from ESA WorldCover 2021, weather from ERA5 reanalysis, and ignition points and reference burn from NASA FIRMS VIIRS detections. Four documented fires in Türkiye cover different fuel, terrain, and wind conditions, namely Sc1 Manavgat and Sc2 Marmaris (July 2021, 48 h), Sc3 Milas-Kemerköy (August 2021, 72 h), and Sc4 Çanakkale-Kayadere (August 2023, 48 h). The core models free spread without suppression. Hence, a free run overpredicts the final area, and three suppression-robust criteria are used. The first is the probability of detection, accepted at 0.70 for calibrated growth models {29}. The second is the front position error relative to the equivalent burn radius. The third is the arrival-time agreement, the direct rate-of-spread test. Table II reports the metrics over 50 replicates and Fig. 5 the agreement maps. Overall agreement is 86 to 94 percent and detection probability 85 to 97 percent."""),

("Synthetic scenarios use", """Synthetic scenarios use 72 by 50 cell grids at 30 m with terrain, land cover, settlements, roads, and capacity-limited bases. The engine runs with a two-minute physical step, a twelve-minute decision cycle, a 24-minute planning horizon, a satisficing bound of 0.35, and a quality gate of 0.60. Episodes run to extinction or to a twelve-hour cap, under critical fire weather (fuel moisture 0.08, wind 8 m/s). Five scenario families span remote forest and the wildland-urban interface under sufficient and limited resources, listed in Table III. The fifth repeats the hardest interface family under degraded observation, with an outage probability of 0.25 and observation noise of 0.10. Each family is generated 30 times from seeds 201 to 230. Every world is replayed identically by three configurations. T0 issues no orders and fixes the cost of inaction. L1 runs the closed layer alone, the five seed rules with their self-tuning. DA is DisasterAware, the closed layer with the open layer on top and the LLM proposer behind the full gate chain. The static seed base without any adaptation (F5) is run as a fourth reference. It is reported where it separates the contribution of the rule-based core from that of adaptation. Terrain, ignitions, weather, and the random stream are shared. Therefore, differences between configurations arise from the issued orders alone. In total there are 600 runs. Outcome is read at the end of the episode on burned area, affected population, the share of incidents extinguished within the episode, and the physical and total cost. Paired statistics report the mean difference on the same worlds with its 95 percent half-width and the number of worlds in which the first configuration is better."""),

("Sensitivity of the closed layer", """Sensitivity of the closed layer to its parameters was assessed one parameter at a time over five worlds, 880 runs in total. Observation quality decides light fire loads, and resources decide heavy ones. The satisficing bound sets how often adaptation engages, 42 percent of cycles at 0.35."""),

("Table IV and Fig. 6 report", """Table IV and Fig. 6 report the outcome per family. Free burn consumes 250.7 ha on average and affects 5793 persons. The rule-based core alone changes this most. The static seed base lowers the burned area to 80.9 ha (paired saving 169.7 ± 10.3 ha, better in all 150 worlds) and the affected population to 494. This gain belongs to the doctrine, not to adaptation. The self-tuning of the closed layer changes the character of the response more than its extent. The share of incidents extinguished within the episode rises from 13 to 52 percent, and the delay term falls from 0.347 to 0.274, since tuned consequents commit the same resources sooner. However, the burned area moves only to 75.4 ha. This is a paired difference of 5.5 ± 7.0 ha that does not separate from zero (better in 73 of 150 worlds). In other words, tuning five rules finishes the fires the doctrine already holds. It does not reach the fires the doctrine does not hold."""),

("DisasterAware does.", """DisasterAware does. Against the closed layer on the same worlds it burns 29.9 ± 7.2 ha less and is better in 128 of 150 worlds, as shown in Fig. 7. There are 13 ties on small fires the doctrine already contained and nine worlds in which it burned more. It raises the share extinguished to 75 percent. It lowers the affected population to 117 persons (274 ± 61 fewer per world, 70 percent below the closed layer). Moreover, it reaches the lowest physical cost (0.081 against 0.139) and the lowest total cost (0.110 against 0.164) of the configurations. The gain is largest where the doctrine is weakest. In the forest family with a reduced pool and two ignitions (S2) the paired difference is 63.6 ± 27.6 ha. In the interface families with two ignitions (S4, S5) DisasterAware burns less in 30 and 29 of 30 worlds and extinguishes 97 and 100 percent of the incidents. Under degraded observation (S5) its burned area moves from 27.2 to 29.2 ha. This is a change of 7 percent inside the confidence interval of either cell, and the configuration remains the best. Hence, reduced observation degrades the response instead of breaking it."""),

("The gain is not bought with resources", """The gain is not bought with resources, as shown in Fig. 8. The response term of DisasterAware (0.308) is below that of the closed layer (0.428) and that of the static doctrine (0.501). The five-term decomposition shows the saving in the physical terms with no growth in the response terms. The rule base reaches 10.4 active rules at the end of an episode under DisasterAware against 10.3 under the closed layer alone. Thus, the margin comes from what the rules say rather than from how many there are. What the open layer costs is computation. An episode makes 10.3 model calls and takes 124 s of wall time against 22 s for the closed layer. This is about two seconds per decision cycle against the twelve minutes available."""),

("Adaptation runs only on demand", """Adaptation runs only on demand. Hence, the volume of trials measures how often the standing decision fell short, and the admission rate measures how strictly the gates filtered the responses. Over the 150 worlds the closed-layer mechanisms inside DisasterAware kept 451 of 2093 consequent-step trials and 469 of 1411 resolution trials. The open layer received 788 proposals, one per call with a single revision, and admitted 338 (43 percent). Fig. 9 shows where the rejections fell. In all, 276 proposals passed the forecast tests but failed the package margin G5, 155 did not improve the forecast at G3, 12 failed the second seed at G4, and 7 were stopped by the syntactic gates. In other words, the model writes well-formed objects, and the simulator decides. Admission depends on the form of the proposal. Macros that blend existing families were admitted in 80 percent of 71 cases, composed interventions in 46 percent of 400, intermediate concepts in 37 percent of 78, and plain rules over the existing vocabulary in 28 percent of 239. Thus, the objects that extend the vocabulary pass more often than new rules inside it."""),

("What was written falls into", """What was written falls into a small number of tactics, each absent from the seed doctrine, as shown in Fig. 10 and Fig. 11. The 67 admitted rules order retardant drops (45), containment lines (39), water drafting (38), and suppression effort (35) far more than the seed base does. Retardant drop and water drafting are not ordered by any seed rule. Hence, the open layer added aerial attack to the doctrine. The 29 admitted concepts are 20 distinct convex combinations that fall into two families. A head-run family joins fire threat with intervention urgency (unreachable head pressure, head run pressure, runaway head momentum). An interface family joins asset exposure with evacuation pressure (interface ember pressure, settlement ember pressure, village interface pressure). The 57 macros reduce to two archetypes. Interface hardening is asset protection with a containment line at 0.7 to 0.8 (14 admissions). The downwind backburn line is a containment line with suppression effort and, at times, resource deployment (15 admissions). The 185 composed interventions, 159 distinct clause sets, are dominated by two geometries. The asset collar is retardant coating of the threatened assets and of the populated cells within two to three cells before the fire arrives (26 admissions in its plain form, 42 with variants). The head-flank retardant band is coating of the head within six cells and of the flanks within three, with or without water drafting at the fire (41 in its two plain forms, 56 with variants). Backfire, wetting, and evacuation clauses are rare and enter only where the forecast rewards them. Fig. 3 shows one such object with its gate values."""),

("Every world starts from the same", """Every world starts from the same five seed rules, and the proposer has no memory across worlds. Hence, an object that recurs in several worlds was rediscovered, not copied. Counting every variant that contains the defining clauses, the asset collar was admitted in 42 distinct worlds, the head-flank retardant band in 51, the interface-hardening macro in 18, the downwind backburn line in 28, and the concept unreachable head pressure, with the same weights, in four. Recurrence across independent worlds is the first evidence that what the open layer learns is structural rather than incidental."""),

("The realized outcome confirms", """The realized outcome confirms the forecast that admitted the objects, as shown in Fig. 12. In the 16 worlds where every proposal was rejected, DisasterAware and the closed layer end within 4.3 ha of each other on average, and most of them are identical. Therefore, the entire margin of Section V-C comes from the 134 worlds with at least one admission, where the mean saving is 32.9 ha. The saving grows with the number of admissions in the world. Moreover, worlds in which a concept or a macro was admitted gain more than worlds with new rules only. This attribution is observational, since the admitted set is not assigned at random. The causal version, replaying each world with one admitted object removed, is left to future work. The single world in Fig. 12(c) shows the mechanism at the scale of one fire. It is an interface world in which the closed layer ends at 74 ha and DisasterAware at 6 ha. The curves separate at minute 14, when one composed intervention coating the threatened assets and the head was admitted."""),

("One decision covers the whole loop", """One decision covers the whole loop, including the two shadow forecasts of the acceptance test and any adaptation that runs. It takes a median 274 ms against a twelve-minute cycle, 909 ms at the ninetieth percentile and 1.28 s at the slowest. The first offensive order is fielded at the first decision, two minutes after ignition. Fig. 13 reports the decision-layer cost for one to sixteen regions at cycles of two, six, and twelve minutes. Every point lies between 0.02 and 0.16 percent of the cycle. Sixteen times the agents cost 1.16 to 1.29 times the share, since each agent reasons over its own region. Six times the forecast horizon raised the median by 1.4 and four times the grid by 6.9. Thus, scalability holds on the axis the architecture is built for, while domain size remains the binding limit."""),

("Against the eight criteria", """Against the eight criteria, the picture is as follows. Predictive fidelity rests on four blind hindcasts with front agreement of 86 to 94 percent. Decision quality follows from the paired comparison, 82 percent less burned area than no action and 40 percent less than the closed layer, better in 128 of 150 paired worlds, with the lowest cost in every family. Timeliness is met with 274 ms per decision and about two seconds of proposer time per cycle. Scalability is met with at most 1.29 times the cost for sixteen times the agents. Robustness is met with a loss of 7 percent under degraded observation while the configuration remains the best tested. Uncertainty handling is structural, since every feature carries a confidence that gates its concept. Adaptability shows in 338 admitted objects that recur across worlds. Explainability shows in every edit, rejection, and generated object being logged with its trigger, gate, and consuming rule. Four criteria are met in full and four on the axis measured here. In detail, the hindcast base covers four fires, outage and noise are tested at one level only, operator-facing interpretability is not evaluated, and uncertainty is carried into the decision but not propagated as an ensemble."""),

("Three lessons transfer", """Three lessons transfer beyond wildfire. First, separating an authoritative hazard model from the decision layer is what makes both testable. The model was validated against satellite records with no decision logic in the loop. The decision layers were then measured against each other on the same worlds. Hence, the benefit of opening the decision space is a paired difference rather than an absolute score. Second, a vocabulary of orders that grows at run time is safe only when admission is decided by gates that do not trust the proposer. More than half of the proposals were rejected, most of them at the package margin after they had passed the forecast tests. That selectivity is why the admitted objects could be fielded and why the outcome improved rather than merely changed. Third, the two layers divide the work by cost and determinism rather than by capability. The moves of the closed layer are deterministic, cheap, and numerous, 3504 trials against 788, and they finish the fires the doctrine already holds. The proposals of the open layer are expensive and not bitwise reproducible, and they reach the fires the doctrine does not hold. A single generative mechanism could in principle write the tuning edits as rules, at roughly four times the model calls of this campaign. However, keeping the deterministic layer underneath minimizes the number of cycles in which the non-reproducible component acts. It also keeps the ledger able to say which change came from tuning and which from generation."""),

("Three limitations bound", """Three limitations bound the claims. The evaluation is simulated. The hazard model is validated on real records, but no order was executed in a real incident and no fire officer took part in the loop. The attribution of the gain to object kinds is observational. A leave-one-out replay of each admitted object, and an ablation that admits the proposals of one kind only, would turn it into a causal account. The proposals of an LLM are not bitwise reproducible. Nevertheless, the campaign is logged with pinned configuration and seeds, and no admission depends on the proposer. A replication with a different model and a repeated run of the same worlds would measure the variance this introduces."""),

("This article presented DisasterAware", """This article presented DisasterAware, a distributed decision support architecture for wildfire response built on two separated cores and two decision layers. The closed layer is a self-tuning fuzzy doctrine over five decision concepts and six intervention families. The open layer lets a pretrained LLM extend that doctrine with rules, concepts, blended interventions, and interventions composed from verified physical effects. A gate chain preserves no-harm, life-safety invariance, coverage, and monotone descent whatever the proposer. On an independently validated core, 150 paired worlds in five scenario families were replayed with no decision support, with the closed layer alone, and with both layers. The open layer admitted 338 of 788 proposals. It burned 29.9 ± 7.2 ha less per world than the closed layer and was better in 128 of 150 worlds. It raised the share of incidents extinguished from 52 to 75 percent and left 70 percent fewer people affected. This was achieved with a smaller committed response, no growth in rule count, and about two seconds of proposer time per decision cycle. The admitted objects recur across independent worlds and reduce to a small set of tactics the seed doctrine did not contain, namely aerial attack, the asset collar, the head-flank retardant band, interface hardening, and the downwind backburn line. Four studies follow. A leave-one-out replay will attribute the gain to each admitted object. A withheld-knowledge study will remove a known tactic from the doctrine and measure its rediscovery. A tabletop exercise will put fire officers in the loop. A transfer study will keep the decision core and replace the hazard model with another spreading hazard."""),
]
